  "articleBody": "Installing wall paneling sounds straightforward until uneven drywall and electrical switches complicate the layout. Avoiding these five common mistakes ensures your trim work looks intentional and lasts for decades.\n\n1. Trusting Out-of-Plumb Corners: Assuming corners are square leads to sloping rails and skewed stiles. Scribe each end piece to fit actual wall contours instead of forcing a 90-degree cut.\n2. Fastening Only into Drywall: Nailing panels solely into drywall board guarantees loose trim over time. Always locate studs and solid blocking for secure fastening.\n3. Overlooking Electrical Outlets: Cutting around switch plates as an afterthought creates awkward gaps. Plan layouts in advance so outlets land cleanly within field panels or get relocated safely by a qualified trade professional.\n4. Skipping Material Acclimation: Installing wood panels right off the truck causes warping. Give wood products at least 48 hours to adjust to your home's indoor humidity.\n5. Measuring Walls Only Once: Floor lines and ceiling heights vary along a single wall. Measure multiple reference heights to calculate exact cuts.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid\nMost finish carpentry issues stem from rushing the layout before checking wall plumb. Taking time to dry-fit parts saves materials and prevents uneven reveals.\n\nIf you want clean, professional wainscoting in the Denver area, get in touch with HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
